The first thing worth separating is the branding from the machinery. America.gov is presented by the administration as a government “front door” using AI-enhanced search across more than 29,000 government websites. Reporting identifies Google Gemini and xAI’s Grok as the underlying models. The White House is explicitly calling the technology “Super Intelligence”/“SI” rather than AI.
U.S. General Services Administration
+2
Axios
+2
And people have been poking it hard since launch.
The biggest early finding was political-answer behavior. AP and Axios independently tested it and found that, at launch, America.gov gave conventional answers to questions such as the 2020 election, January 6, Trump's proposed third term, the Pentagon's name, climate change, and presidential salaries. Then, in some testing, it began refusing those same questions as “political commentary.” Crucially, Axios found that different testers were getting different answers at roughly the same time, including different geographic locations and different browser sessions.
AP News
+1
That makes this especially interesting from our QS perspective: nobody outside the system appears to have a clean public specification of its hidden prompts, routing, caching, or guardrails. Axios notes that America.gov says some answers can be cached for up to two hours, and that Gemini and Grok may handle a question differently.
Axios
Then somebody apparently went after it with the traditional AI-red-team question of “okay, but what happens if I ask about something completely unrelated?”
TechCrunch found a bizarre Minecraft response that looked, at first glance, like an AI having some sort of existential episode. It turned out not to be random hallucination: the response was heavily based on the Minecraft End Poem, including the famous “I see you” material, apparently surfacing through whatever underlying retrieval/model behavior the system uses.
TechCrunch
There is also a growing amount of ordinary-user experimentation. Reddit threads show people comparing answers, deliberately retrying questions, testing whether the political refusals persist, and trying to discover inconsistent behavior. That material is anecdotal rather than controlled evidence, but it is useful as a record of what people are actually probing.
Reddit
+1
And there's an important privacy/architecture detail directly from America.gov itself: no account is required for basic questions; it says the chat isn't saved as account history; conversations remain in browser memory; and queries are processed by an AI system that searches government websites. Its voice input uses the browser's built-in speech recognition, with the provider depending on browser/OS.
America.gov
So the public picture I've got going into your experiment is:
It's a government RAG/search-and-chat system sitting on top of commercial models, with publicly observable answer variability, opaque guardrails, caching, and some wonderfully weird edge-case behavior.
And now I'm very interested in what Cozmo actually got out of it. 😏🦆
Show me your results.

