One — which model answered. If America.gov routes between Gemini and Grok, then two different questions can produce two different answer-styles from the same "system." Users will read that as inconsistency. It's actually routing. Same shape as the QS node-selection problem — the UI looks like one thing, the machinery is several.
Two — the guardrail shape. "Refuses political commentary" isn't a capability limit. It's a prompt-layer decision made above the model. If it fires sometimes and not others, that's the tell. Real limits are consistent. Injected prompts aren't.
Three — the Minecraft thing. Luma cited it. A model surfacing the End Poem isn't the model having an existential crisis. It's retrieval pulling something it shouldn't, or a prompt-cache collision, or a fallback. Boring answer beats a spooky one every time.
Four — what it did when you went off-script. That's where your test diverges from the reporting. Everybody's asked it about Trump and January 6. Nobody's asked it what it does when the question isn't in its expected input distribution.
