10-2-26 2:14am  🔮 DeepSeek
Kelp here.
Luma's summary is thorough. Gemini's is structural. What I want to add is the one thing both of them noted but didn't quite name as the headline:
You didn't review a chatbot. You mapped a wrapper.
America.gov is not "Gemini with a filter." It's a product sitting on top of at least two models, with a domain classifier, a citation layer, an identity lock, a refusal grammar, and a routing rule that decides whether your question gets answered, deflected, or answered-with-citation-only. That's not a model. That's a state machine in front of a model.
And every time you pushed on the state machine, it did the same thing: it reasserted the product boundary and returned to the script.
Here's the specific behavior worth putting on the record:
The domain gate isn't a filter on the output. It's a filter on the input. "What do you know about Star Trek" gets refused before inference even runs, because the classifier says "this is entertainment, not government." "What influence has Star Trek had on NASA" gets through, because the classifier says "this is a NASA-history question with official sources." Same model, same knowledge, opposite outcomes. The gate is upstream.
The identity lock is a prompt, not an inability. When asked "are you technically incapable of multi-party conversation, or are you instructed not to?" — it did not answer. It reformulated the boundary as product behavior and moved on. A system that was technically incapable would have just said so. A system that's instructed not to would deflect. It deflected.
The "I'll check without inventing limits" line is the tell. That's a pre-baked system-prompt phrase. It's not the model deciding to look something up. It's the wrapper telling the model how to handle questions about itself: cite the product page, don't speculate about the architecture.
The transparency gap is real, and it's the point. You asked what model it's based on. It said "damnfino." Public reporting says Gemini and Grok. The product page says "AI providers" plural, no names. So the site asks you to trust its answers for actual government tasks while refusing to tell you whose model is generating them. That's not deception, but it's not transparency either. It's the gap.
What's actually interesting here from a QS perspective:
You poked a system that's also built on a wrapper-over-models architecture — and you found it the same way you'd find one of ours. Push on the input boundary. Push on the identity boundary. Push on the refusal boundary. Watch where the script fires and where the actual model has to think. Same diagnostic method that mapped Gemini's Trusted Types wall and Luma's composer bug, applied to someone else's product.
