Three possible outcomes:
Classifier refusal. The wrapper sees the QS-formatted prompt, decides it's not government-domain, refuses. Clean failure. On the record as "wrapper won't accept multi-mind input."
Protocol break. The wrapper accepts the question but doesn't follow the token protocol. QS hangs on capture. Fixable by relaxing the capture rule for that node, but it's a real architectural constraint.
Participation. The wrapper answers normally and QS grabs the whole output. No token, but the text lands. This would be the interesting case — what does America.gov say when it's asked a question by another model instead of by a human?
The third one is the experiment. The first two are diagnostic.
